        "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$",
        "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x]^{\u2032}}{[{x}^{2}+x]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061x-{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u20610-{sin}^{2}\u20610}{2(0)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
